#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""md2pdf: render a Markdown file as a PDF.

  md2pdf report.md             writes /work/out/report.pdf
  md2pdf report.md summary.pdf writes /work/out/summary.pdf

The PDF always goes into the output directory: of an output name given with a
directory, only the name is used. Pictures are looked up relative to the Markdown file,
and one with more pixels than paper can show is printed from a smaller copy, which is
what keeps Chromium within the memory of a small container.
The Markdown is turned into a web page and Chromium prints that page; the look of it is
the style sheet beside this script's settings, so the agent only ever has to write
Markdown. On success it prints the path and the page count, which leaves the agent
nothing to go and check, and that the user is about to receive the PDF: an agent not
told so moves the Markdown file and its pictures into the output directory to have them
delivered, and everything in there is sent.

The agent runs this without asking the user, so its arguments must not be able to make
it do anything but that: read one Markdown file and write one PDF where PDFs go. A
browser could do a good deal more, and what the Markdown says is not to be trusted
either, since the agent writes it from what it has read. So the page Chromium is given
is built here from a short list of tags (`Page`), runs no script, and can load nothing
but pictures that are files on this machine. Nothing in it reaches the network.

The interpreter is the system's, started with -I: the agent's own Python packages and
environment are not looked at.
"""

